-- title:
--   Proposition 4.6 — conditional Gaussian coupling of signed columns and Brownian increments
-- statement:
--   Let $B$ be an $M$-dimensional Brownian motion, $t_k=k/N$, $\mathcal G^B_k=\sigma(B_{t_0},\dots,B_{t_k})$, and let $\mathcal H_0$ be a $\sigma$-field independent of its future increments. Suppose $b_k$ is $\mathcal H_0\vee\mathcal G^B_k$-measurable with $\|b_k\|_2\le c_G/\sqrt N$, $0\le k<N$. Iterating the inverse map in Lemma 2.5 produces independent vectors $Z_{k+1}\sim N(0,I_M/N)$ and online signs $\varepsilon_{k+1}$ such that
--   $$
--   \mathbb E[\varepsilon_{k+1}Z_{k+1}\mid\mathcal H_0\vee\mathcal G^B_k]=b_k .
--   $$
--   After adjoining $\mathcal H_0$, the completed $\sigma$-fields generated by $Z_1,\dots,Z_k$ and by $B_{t_0},\dots,B_{t_k}$ agree. For every $p>2$ there is a constant $C_p$, independent of $M$ and $N$, such that for $0\le k_0<k_1\le N$,
--   $$
--   \mathbb E\Big[\Big\|\sum_{k=k_0}^{k_1-1}(\varepsilon_{k+1}Z_{k+1}-b_k)-(B_{t_{k_1}}-B_{t_{k_0}})\Big\|_\infty\ \Big|\ \mathcal H_0\Big]\le C_pN^{1/4}\max_{k_0\le k<k_1}\Big(\sum_{i=1}^M\mathbb E[|b_{k,i}|^p\mid\mathcal H_0]\Big)^{1/(2p)}\quad\text{a.s.}
--   $$
--
--   This coupling keeps the signed partial sums of Algorithm 1 close to their Brownian counterparts uniformly over all $M$ coordinates.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: "For every p > 2 there is a constant C_p, independent of M and N" → ∀ p > 2, ∃ C_p, ∀ M N, ∀ probability space (in Type), B, H₀, drifts b: all clauses; reason: C_p uniform in M, N, H₀ and the drifts, as the proof gives (it depends only on p); the clauses without p are stated under the same quantifiers
--   - reading: H₀ independent of the future increments of B → H₀ independent of σ(B_i(t) − B_i(s) : i ≤ M, s, t ∈ [0, 1]); reason: the coupling starts at time 0, so every increment on [0, 1] is in the future
--   - reading: "online signs ε_{k+1}" → ε_{k+1} is ±1-valued and measurable for the completion of H₀ ∨ σ(Z₁, …, Z_{k+1}); reason: the sign depends only on the past and the current input
--   - encoding: conditional expectations, ‖·‖_∞, max over k₀ ≤ k < k₁; 0-based indices → condExp given the sub-σ-algebra H₀ (resp. H₀ ⊔ meshSigma); the sup norm on ℝ^M; Finset.sup'; coupledZ N b B k for Z_{k+1}; reason: the same quantities
-- source:
--   Jo, Lee, The Exact Online Threshold for the Asymmetric Binary Perceptron, arXiv:2609.02124 (artifact sha256 bd5e2bdb18a47b7e806191ae9b888a6e4e7e150a9bd50e541b7587007d07a7a2), Proposition 4.6, (23)–(24), p.15 (PDF p.16); proof in Appendix B, pp.33–36

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ABPOnline_IsBrownianVec
import Definitions.Def_ABPOnline_augmentSigma
import Definitions.Def_ABPOnline_coupledZ

set_option autoImplicit false

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory
open scoped ENNReal NNReal

namespace ABPOnline

/-- **Proposition 4.6 (Conditional Gaussian coupling)** (arXiv:2609.02124, printed p.15, PDF p.16).
Let `B` be an `M`-dimensional Brownian motion, and let `H₀` be a σ-field independent of its future
increments. Suppose `b_k ∈ L⁰(H₀ ∨ G^B_k; ℝ^M)`, `‖b_k‖₂ ≤ c_G/√N`, `0 ≤ k < N`. Iterating the
inverse map in Lemma 2.5 produces independent vectors `Z_{k+1} ∼ N(0, I_M/N)` and online signs
`ε_{k+1}` such that `E[ε_{k+1}Z_{k+1} | H₀ ∨ G^B_k] = b_k`. After adjoining `H₀`, the completed
σ-fields generated by `Z_1, …, Z_k` and by `B_{t_0}, …, B_{t_k}` agree. For every `p > 2` there is a
constant `C_p`, independent of `M` and `N`, such that for `0 ≤ k₀ < k₁ ≤ N`, (24):
`E[‖∑_{k=k₀}^{k₁−1} (ε_{k+1}Z_{k+1} − b_k) − (B_{t_{k₁}} − B_{t_{k₀}})‖_∞ | H₀]
  ≤ C_p N^{1/4} max_{k₀≤k<k₁} (∑_{i=1}^M E[|b_{k,i}|^p | H₀])^{1/(2p)}` a.s.

**Formalization Note** The statement is `∀ p > 2, ∃ C_p, ∀ M N, ∀ (Ω, P, B, H₀, b)`, so `C_p` is
uniform in `M`, `N`, the probability space, `H₀` and the drifts; the clauses that do not involve
`p` are stated under the same quantifiers. Probability spaces are in `Type`. "Independent of its
future increments" is independence of `H₀` from all increments `B_i(t) − B_i(s)`, `s, t ∈ [0,1]`. "Online signs":
`ε_{k+1}` is measurable for the completion of `H₀ ∨ σ(Z_1, …, Z_{k+1})`. The norm `‖·‖` on `ℝ^M`
is the sup norm. Indices are 0-based: `coupledZ N b B k` is `Z_{k+1}`. -/
theorem proposition_4_6 (p : ℝ) (hp : 2 < p) :
    ∃ Cp : ℝ, ∀ (M N : ℕ) (Ω : Type) (H₀ : MeasurableSpace Ω) [mΩ : MeasurableSpace Ω]
      (P : Measure Ω) [IsProbabilityMeasure P] (B : Fin M → ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ)
      (b : ℕ → Ω → Fin M → ℝ),
      H₀ ≤ mΩ → IsBrownianVec B P →
      Indep H₀ (⨆ (i : Fin M) (s : ℝ≥0) (t : ℝ≥0) (_ : s ≤ 1 ∧ t ≤ 1),
        MeasurableSpace.comap (fun ω => B i t ω - B i s ω) inferInstance) P →
      (∀ k < N, Measurable[H₀ ⊔ meshSigma B N k] (b k)) →
      (∀ k < N, ∀ ω, l2norm (b k ω) ≤ cG / Real.sqrt N) →
      iIndepFun (fun k : Fin N => coupledZ N b B k) P ∧
      (∀ k < N, P.map (coupledZ N b B k) =
        Measure.pi fun _ : Fin M => gaussianReal 0 (N : ℝ≥0)⁻¹) ∧
      (∀ k < N, (∀ ω, coupledSign N b B k ω = 1 ∨ coupledSign N b B k ω = -1) ∧
        Measurable[augmentSigma P (H₀ ⊔ coupledZSigma N b B (k + 1))] (coupledSign N b B k)) ∧
      (∀ k < N, ∀ i : Fin M,
        P[fun ω => coupledSign N b B k ω * coupledZ N b B k ω i | H₀ ⊔ meshSigma B N k]
          =ᵐ[P] fun ω => b k ω i) ∧
      (∀ k ≤ N, augmentSigma P (H₀ ⊔ coupledZSigma N b B k) =
        augmentSigma P (H₀ ⊔ meshSigma B N k)) ∧
      ∀ k₀ k₁ : ℕ, (hk : k₀ < k₁) → k₁ ≤ N → ∀ᵐ ω ∂P,
        (P[fun ω' => ‖(∑ k ∈ Finset.Ico k₀ k₁,
            (coupledSign N b B k ω' • coupledZ N b B k ω' - b k ω')) -
            (meshVal B N k₁ ω' - meshVal B N k₀ ω')‖ | H₀]) ω ≤
          Cp * (N : ℝ) ^ (1 / 4 : ℝ) *
            (Finset.Ico k₀ k₁).sup' (Finset.nonempty_Ico.2 hk)
              (fun k => (∑ i : Fin M, (P[fun ω' => |b k ω' i| ^ p | H₀]) ω) ^ (1 / (2 * p))) := by sorry

end ABPOnline
